---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import BahdanauReadLedger from "./BahdanauReadLedger.astro";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import GravesWindowComparison from "./GravesWindowComparison.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const expert = mode === "expert";
const reads = view.bahdanauReads;
const main = reads?.find((s) => s.case === "changing-query");
if (reads?.length !== 3 || main === undefined) throw new Error("Bahdanau requires its read and both reviewed safeguards.");
const journey = getJourneyOverview("encoder-decoder-alignment");
const claim = (id: string) => {
  const e = view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === `bahdanau-2014-${id}`);
  if (e === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing Bahdanau evidence: ${id}`);
  return e;
};
const math = (s: string) => renderToString(s, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const results = [
  ["RNNencdec-30", "13.93", "24.19"], ["RNNsearch-30", "21.50", "31.44"],
  ["RNNencdec-50", "17.82", "26.71"], ["RNNsearch-50", "26.75", "34.16"],
  ["RNNsearch-50* · longer trained", "28.45", "36.15"], ["Moses", "33.30", "35.63"],
];
const training = [["RNNenc-30", "109"], ["RNNenc-50", "108"], ["RNNsearch-30", "113"], ["RNNsearch-50", "111"], ["RNNsearch-50*", "252"]];
---
<article class="stage-shell alignment-stage">
  <div class="stage-reading">
    <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
    <header class="stage-header"><p class="stage-number">Stage 3 of 4 · first preprint September 1, 2014 · ICLR 2015</p><h1>{expert ? "Reconstruct the read. Audit the evidence." : "How can the decoder revisit the source?"}</h1><p class="stage-question">{view.contribution}</p><dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>{view.work.title} · Bahdanau, Cho and Bengio</dd></div><div><dt>Display source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label} · v5 arithmetic</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Recurrent decoder · contextual source annotations · soft weighted read</dd></div></dl></header>
    <section class="objective-band" aria-labelledby="objective-heading"><h2 id="objective-heading">Your task</h2><p>{view.objective.objective}</p><details><summary>Success criterion</summary><p>{view.objective.successCriterion}</p></details></section>
    <nav class="preset-index mt-4" aria-label="Bahdanau chapter sections"><a href="#bahdanau-bank-heading">Source bank</a><a href="#bahdanau-two-reads-heading">Two reads</a><a href="#bahdanau-safeguards">Safeguards</a><a href="#graves-window-comparison">Window comparison</a><a href="#bahdanau-results">Paper results</a></nav>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-bank-heading"><p class="kicker">Architecture · one bank, repeated reads</p><h2 id="bahdanau-bank-heading">Keep the source available at every step.</h2><p>Cho and Sutskever’s chapters use one source summary for the target sequence. Bahdanau, Cho and Bengio propose keeping an annotation at each source position and forming a new weighted combination for each target word. Each annotation joins states from a forward and a backward recurrent encoder, so it includes context from both sides of its source word.</p><p class="mt-3">The annotation bank grows with source length; each context vector keeps the same width. The backward encoder needs the whole source. This architecture does not encode a live stream one word at a time.</p>{!expert && <p class="mt-3">Think of returning to a page of reading notes before writing the next word. The actual operation is more precise: it mixes numerical vectors rather than picking a sentence or copying a word. A position’s annotation includes surrounding context, not just the word printed beside it.</p>}<EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("bidirectional-annotations")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("soft-attention-context")} /></section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-order-heading"><p class="kicker">Main-text abstraction · read before update</p><h2 id="bahdanau-order-heading">Three operations, with two normalization axes.</h2><ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Bahdanau main-text operation order"><li><strong>Read from the prior state</strong><span>Score every source annotation using sᵢ₋₁; normalize across source positions; sum the weighted vectors into cᵢ.</span></li><li><strong>Update the recurrent state</strong><span>Use the prior state, previous target word and context to form sᵢ.</span></li><li><strong>Predict a target word</strong><span>Use sᵢ, cᵢ and the previous word to form a separate distribution over the target vocabulary.</span></li></ol>
      {expert && <div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Bahdanau v5 source-read and conditional-output abstraction" tabindex="0"><div set:html={math(String.raw`e_{ij}=v_a^\top\tanh(W_as_{i-1}+U_ah_j),\quad\alpha_{ij}=\frac{\exp(e_{ij})}{\sum_{k=1}^{T_x}\exp(e_{ik})}`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`c_i=\sum_{j=1}^{T_x}\alpha_{ij}h_j,\quad s_i=f(s_{i-1},y_{i-1},c_i)`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`p(y\mid x)=\prod_i p(y_i\mid y_{<i},x),\quad p(y_i\mid y_{<i},x)=g(y_{i-1},s_i,c_i)`)} /></div>}
      <p class="mt-3">Energy is a raw compatibility score. The source softmax turns those scores into nonnegative shares summing to one. The target-vocabulary softmax answers a different question: which word to produce. The context is a vector, not either probability distribution.</p><p class="mt-3">Training maximizes conditional translation likelihood using observed prefixes. Differentiability lets translation-loss gradients train the encoder, scorer and decoder jointly, without supervised word-alignment labels. Generation uses its own prefixes and approximate beam search. The paper does not report beam width, length normalization or stopping details.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("conditional-objective")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("differentiable-soft-alignment")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("beam-search-boundary")} />
      <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check the two denominators</summary><p>The source-read denominator spans source positions for a fixed target step. It cannot give P(next word = X). That probability belongs to the output distribution over target-vocabulary items. No target word is generated by the teaching read.</p></details>
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-extension-heading"><p class="kicker">A framework extension · bounded evidence</p><h2 id="bahdanau-extension-heading">A new read does not prove a universal bottleneck.</h2><p>The paper conjectures that one fixed-length source summary can be a bottleneck, especially on longer sentences. It explicitly extends the encoder–decoder framework and attributes its gated units to Cho. Fixing every context to the terminal forward encoder state recovers Cho’s fixed-context form in the source’s architectural description.</p><p class="mt-3">That equation-level comparison is not a matched experiment. RNNsearch also adds a backward encoder and more capacity. Sutskever’s successful reversal branch remains a counterexample to a blanket claim that every fixed-summary system fails on long sentences.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("fixed-vector-bottleneck-hypothesis")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cho-framework-extension")} />
    </section>
  </div>
  <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long alignment-workbench" aria-label="Complete static Bahdanau source read"><BahdanauReadLedger scenario={main} mode={mode} /></aside>
  <div class="stage-conclusion bahdanau-conclusion">
    <section class="reading-block alignment-boundaries" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-safeguards"><p class="kicker">Check the invariants</p><h2 id="bahdanau-safeguards">A new step need not change the context.</h2><p>The following complete static cases use the same supplied states. The first changes only the score-output vector; the second changes only the annotation bank. Inspect both reads before checking the prediction. The arithmetic details retain all inputs, intermediate terms and independently checked expected outputs in both modes.</p>{expert && <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check common-offset invariance</summary><p>Adding the same constant to all source energies leaves every weight unchanged: the common exponential factor cancels between numerator and denominator. Subtracting the maximum in each displayed calculation uses this identity. It does not change the scorer or its source normalization axis.</p></details>}</section>
    <div class="alignment-evidence bahdanau-safeguards">{reads.filter((s) => s.case !== "changing-query").map((s) => <BahdanauReadLedger scenario={s} mode={mode} />)}</div>
    <div class="alignment-evidence">
      <GravesWindowComparison view={view} />
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-version-heading"><p class="kicker">Source audit · preserve the conflicts</p><h2 id="bahdanau-version-heading">One stable read, differing decoder indices.</h2><p>Both v5 and canonical v7 retain the main-text sequence: score from sᵢ₋₁, read cᵢ, update sᵢ, then predict yᵢ. The synthetic calculation evaluates only the source read and supplies both prior states independently. The second state is not computed from the first read.</p><dl class="alignment-version-list mt-4"><div><dt>v5 · mixed appendix</dt><dd>A.1.1 uses yᵢ in gate inputs beside yᵢ₋₁ in the candidate; A.2.2 omits W before the candidate’s target embedding. Other detailed indices also differ. These are not combined into one reconstructed decoder.</dd></div><div><dt>v6 · different state convention</dt><dd>Much of the detailed decoder uses a post-output state convention, while a conditional mismatch remains. The read’s prior-state abstraction stays separately scoped.</dd></div><div><dt>v7 · partial repairs</dt><dd>The gate inputs and candidate matrix are repaired, but the detailed output prose/readout use sᵢ₋₁ while its conditional prints sᵢ. Canonical does not mean every equation is consistent.</dd></div><div><dt>Update gate z</dt><dd>Here z weights the candidate in (1 − z) × old + z × candidate. Cho’s main paper uses z for the old-state contribution. A familiar unit name does not fix the symbol’s role.</dd></div></dl><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("equation-version-boundary")} />
      <p class="mt-4">The first Bahdanau preprint, posted September 1, 2014, already names Sutskever in the body and lists an anonymized, under-review reference. Sutskever’s September 10 first preprint reciprocally cites Bahdanau. Posting order cannot establish conception, submission or private-circulation order; the corpus asserts no directed Sutskever–Bahdanau relationship.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("sutskever-cross-citation-boundary")} /></section>
    </div>
    <section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-results"><p class="kicker">Canonical Table 1 · article p. 7</p><h2 id="bahdanau-results">Read the evaluation condition before the score.</h2><p>BLEU compares produced translations with reference wording; a higher value is better under the same evaluation conditions. These are WMT’14 English-to-French test scores. All evaluates the full 3,003-sentence set. No UNK keeps only sentences whose source and reference contain no unknown tokens, and also forbids neural decoders from emitting [UNK]. Its subset size is not reported. These columns are different evaluation conditions.</p><table class="trace-table alignment-results bahdanau-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 1 · complete All and No UNK BLEU</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">All</th><th scope="col">No UNK</th></tr></thead><tbody>{results.map(([label, all, known]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{all}</td><td>{known}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">The longer-trained RNNsearch-50* is 4.85 BLEU below Moses on All, and 0.52 above it on No UNK. Moses additionally uses a 418-million-word monolingual corpus; the neural systems use parallel data only. No repeated-seed uncertainty or significance test accompanies these scores.</p><details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check the claim behind 36.15</summary><p>It is the longer-trained model on the filtered No UNK condition with unknown outputs disallowed. It is not the full-set result, and does not show that rare-word handling was solved. Both vocabularies still have a 30,000-word shortlist.</p></details><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("bleu-results")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("unknown-word-boundary")} /></section>
    <div class="alignment-evidence">
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-conditions"><p class="kicker">Experiment envelope</p><h2 id="bahdanau-conditions">Length caps, capacity and time change the comparison.</h2><p>The study selects 348 million words from an 850-million-word parallel pool. Development combines news-test-2012 and news-test-2013; the test uses 3,003 unseen news-test-2014 sentences. Separate source and target shortlists retain 30,000 words each; everything else maps to [UNK]. The accepted v5 corrects one crawled-corpus component to 272.5 million; v1–v4 print 780 million while still claiming an 850-million total.</p><p class="mt-3">RNNencdec has a 1,000-unit encoder and decoder. RNNsearch has two 1,000-unit encoder directions and a 1,000-unit decoder. Embeddings have 620 coordinates; the maxout layer has 500 units; the alignment hidden layer has 1,000. The compared systems are not parameter-matched.</p><p class="mt-3">The 30/50 suffixes are training sentence-pair length caps. Training uses Adadelta with ρ = 0.95, ε = 10⁻⁶, batches of 80 and gradient-norm clipping at 1. Table 2 uses the shorter name RNNenc for the comparator.</p><table class="trace-table mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 2 · all reported training durations</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Hours</th></tr></thead><tbody>{training.map(([label, hours]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{hours}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">The starred run trains for more than twice the ordinary search-50 wall time. Table 2 warns that likelihoods across length-capped rows are not directly comparable. Wall time here is not an equal-compute experiment.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("data-and-vocabulary")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("architecture-condition")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("training-condition")} /></section>
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="bahdanau-evidence-boundary"><p class="kicker">What the evidence can establish</p><h2 id="bahdanau-evidence-boundary">A useful read, with limits on interpretation.</h2><p>Figure 2 shows the fixed-context curves deteriorating with length while RNNsearch is more robust; the authors describe no visible deterioration for search-50 at 50 or more words. The figure provides no exact points, bin counts or uncertainty, and leaves the source-versus-target length definition unstated. It does not isolate compression from bidirectionality, capacity or training caps.</p><p class="mt-3">The four attention matrices illustrate mostly monotonic alignments and some reordering. One is arbitrary; three are drawn from test sentences of 10–20 words with no unknowns. They are qualitative examples, without gold alignment labels, alignment-error rate or a causal intervention. An importance weight is not a validated explanation or alignment posterior.</p><p class="mt-3">Every context sums all source annotations: peaked weights do not skip computation. There are Tₓ × Tᵧ alignment evaluations per source–target pair. The paper calls this tolerable for typical 15–40-word translation pairs while noting a possible cost limit elsewhere.</p><p class="mt-3">The study covers one language direction and test set. It supplies no matched component-only ablation, repeated-seed uncertainty or full decoding configuration. The static examples demonstrate neither learned alignment nor a BLEU gain. This is recurrent decoder-to-source retrieval; it is not Transformer self-attention.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("length-results")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("soft-alignment-interpretation-boundary")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("global-attention-cost")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("evidence-boundary")} /><a class="claim-link" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete nineteen-claim Bahdanau record</a></section>
    </div>
    <nav class="reading-block alignment-progression" aria-label="Alignment journey progression"><p class="kicker">Next distinction · chapter queued</p><h2>Which state is scored, and which source positions are read?</h2><p>Luong’s later chapter will compare current-state scoring, global versus local support, and input feeding. It remains queued. Its corpus record retains the source and evaluation boundaries; no numerical local-p window is implied by this chapter.</p><a class="claim-link" href={`/atlas/${mode}/#work-luong-pham-manning-2015-effective-attention-nmt`}>Inspect Luong’s inline corpus record</a><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button--quiet" href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/sutskever-2014/`}>Return to Sutskever in {mode} mode</a><a href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a></div><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={view.stage.slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
  </div>
</article>
